WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.571
What changed in 2026-07-28: MCP became stateless.

2
00:00:06.851 --> 00:00:15.960
The revision current on this lesson’s review date is 2026-07-28, and it changed the protocol’s foundations.

3
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.701
If you learned MCP from material written in 2025, three changes matter.

4
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:23.176
No more handshake.

5
00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:31.264
Earlier revisions opened every connection with an initialize exchange that fixed the protocol version and capabilities for the session.

6
00:00:31.544 --> 00:00:38.968
Now every request carries its protocol version and the client’s capabilities in its _meta field, under keys such as io.

7
00:00:39.248 --> 00:00:42.427
modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion.

8
00:00:42.707 --> 00:00:50.749
A server accepts or rejects each request on its own; an unsupported version gets an error listing the versions it does support.

9
00:00:51.029 --> 00:00:58.453
Servers must also implement server/discover, which a client may call first to learn versions and capabilities up front.

10
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:00.675
No server-initiated requests.

11
00:01:00.955 --> 00:01:07.163
Earlier servers could send requests to the client in the middle of a call (to elicit, sample, or list roots).

12
00:01:07.443 --> 00:01:14.205
Now a server that needs input returns a result with resultType set to input_required, listing what it needs.

13
00:01:14.485 --> 00:01:19.243
The client gathers the answers and retries the original request with them attached.

14
00:01:19.523 --> 00:01:23.128
The changelog calls this the multi round-trip request pattern.

15
00:01:23.408 --> 00:01:26.821
Every result now carries a resultType, normally complete.

16
00:01:27.101 --> 00:01:29.619
No protocol sessions on HTTP.

17
00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:39.221
The Mcp-Session-Id header is gone; servers that need state across calls mint explicit handles and pass them as ordinary tool arguments.

18
00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:41.443
You rarely write this plumbing yourself.

19
00:01:41.723 --> 00:01:53.968
The official Python SDK’s v2 release (July 2026) supports the new revision and still serves 2025-era clients from the same server object.

20
00:01:54.248 --> 00:02:02.504
But you will read logs, debug with the Inspector, and evaluate third-party servers, and knowing which era you are looking at saves confusion.
